WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Remote command execution has extra complexity because there may be local Bash interpretation and remote environment interpretation.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.320
A value can be processed locally before the remote side receives it, and then interpreted again according to the remote command or shell behavior.

3
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.960
Secure scripts avoid arbitrary remote command text.

4
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:26.100
A safer design uses fixed remote actions, controlled scripts, validated operands, and clear argument boundaries.

5
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.600
The remote side should expose known behavior when possible, rather than accepting open-ended instructions from a local wrapper.

6
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.880
Values that affect remote commands should be validated before use.

7
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:44.900
Host labels, service names, file paths, modes, and environment names should have supported shapes and meanings.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.960
Quoting and structured argument handling help preserve accepted values as data rather than accidental syntax.

9
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.640
The script should check connection results and remote command results before continuing.

10
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:13.140
A connection failure, host trust failure, authentication failure, remote command failure, timeout, or unexpected output should not be reported as success simply because the local script reached the next line.

11
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.980
Timeouts and retry limits matter for unattended remote administration.

12
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.880
A hung remote command can block a maintenance window.

13
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.780
Repeated retries can amplify a problem.

14
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:30.520
Clear failure messages help operators distinguish connectivity, authorization, and remote execution issues.

15
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.880
The goal is a remote action that can be reviewed and trusted.

16
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:41.700
Known remote behaviors with validated inputs are easier to operate than a general-purpose remote shell hidden inside a Bash automation script.
